course: books
lesson: 53
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter VIII, part 2: Toad's Adventures (the washerwoman's dress)
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: One morning the gaoler's daughter is thoughtful: «I have an aunt who is a washerwoman.» Toad, not really listening, jokes rudely that he has several aunts who ought to be washerwomen, and she tells him he talks too much.
explain: Her plan: the aunt does the prisoners' washing, taking it out on Monday morning and bringing it back on Friday evening; if Toad pays her well, she will give him her dress and bonnet, and he can walk out of the castle as the washerwoman.
explain: Toad is offended — Mr. Toad of Toad Hall, dressed as a washerwoman! — but when the girl snaps that he can stay in prison as a Toad, he admits he has been proud and stupid.
explain: Next evening the aunt comes; for some gold sovereigns she hands over a cotton print gown, an apron, a shawl and a rusty black bonnet, and asks to be tied up and gagged, so it will look as if she was attacked.
explain: Dressed up and giggled at, Toad walks out past gate after gate, the warders joking with the «washerwoman» all the way, until the little gate clicks behind him and he feels the fresh air: he is free!
fact: The girl says: «I have an aunt who is a washerwoman»; Toad answers: «I have several aunts who ought to be washerwomen» — a rude joke, as if washing work were beneath his family: an old snobbish idea.
fact: She tells him: «You talk too much, that's your chief fault, and I'm trying to think, and you hurt my head.»
fact: The aunt «does the washing for all the prisoners in this castle»; she takes it out «on Monday morning, and brings it in on Friday evening». The girl says: «This is a Thursday.»
fact: The aunt could be «properly approached — squared, I believe is the word you animals use»: «squared» is old slang for paid secretly to help (bribed).
fact: «You're very alike in many respects — particularly about the figure.» Toad, huffy: «I have a very elegant figure — for what I am.» She answers: «So has my aunt, for what she is.»
fact: Toad protests: «you wouldn't surely have Mr. Toad of Toad Hall, going about the country disguised as a washerwoman!»
fact: She snaps: «Then you can stop here as a Toad ... I suppose you want to go off in a coach-and-four!» (a grand carriage pulled by four horses).
fact: «Honest Toad was always ready to admit himself in the wrong»: «You are a good, kind, clever girl ... and I am indeed a proud and a stupid toad.»
fact: Next evening the aunt comes; «certain gold sovereigns» (gold coins worth a pound each), placed where she can see them, «practically completed the matter».
fact: For his money Toad gets «a cotton print gown, an apron, a shawl, and a rusty black bonnet».
fact: The aunt asks to be «gagged and bound and dumped down in a corner», so she can say she was attacked and keep her job; the book calls this a «not very convincing artifice» (artifice = trick).
fact: Toad likes that: he can leave «in some style», with his «reputation for being a desperate and dangerous fellow» unspoilt.
fact: The girl «hook-and-eyes» him into the gown (fastens it with little hooks), laughing: «You're the very image of her ... only I'm sure you never looked half so respectable in all your life before.»
fact: On the way out the warders «chaff» him (tease him) with jokes; the book's jokes about men and a «widow woman» come from old ideas about men and women. Toad keeps his temper, though he finds their jokes «poor and clumsy».
fact: Toad felt «a little humbled», because all the friendly greetings were really meant for the washerwoman, not for him.
fact: At last «the wicket-gate» (a small door set in a big gate) clicks behind him, he feels «the fresh air of the outer world», «and knew that he was free!»
fact: Talk in this lesson: «bare» (London) = very; «wicked» (New England) = really; «tamping» (Wales) = furious; «proper job» (West Country) = well done; «rizz» = charm.
exercise: What does the girl say about her aunt? | She is a washerwoman | She is a cook at the castle | She is a magistrate's wife | She sells toast at the market
exercise: What rude joke does Toad make about it? | He has several aunts who ought to be washerwomen | His aunts are far too grand to speak to a washerwoman | Washerwomen should all be locked in the castle too | His own aunt once washed the Queen's best socks
exercise: What does the girl call Toad's «chief fault»? | He talks too much | He eats too much toast | He sleeps too late | He sings out of tune
exercise: Whose washing does the aunt do? | All the prisoners' washing | Only the gaoler's own shirts and sheets | The policemen's helmets and their boots | The washing of the whole town on market day
exercise: When does the aunt take the washing out, and bring it back? | Out on Monday morning, back on Friday evening | Out on Friday evening, back on Monday morning | Out every single day and back the same night | Out once a month, on the first Sunday
exercise: What day is it when the girl explains her plan? | Thursday | Monday | Friday | Sunday
exercise: What does «squared» mean in the girl's plan? | Paid secretly to help | Made to stand very still | Measured for a new dress | Locked in a square cell
exercise: Why does the girl think the plan could work? | Toad and the aunt look alike, especially in figure | The aunt is a magician who can make Toad vanish into thin air | The guards are all asleep on Thursdays | Toad can climb the castle walls in a dress
exercise: «I have a very elegant figure — for what I am.» What does the girl answer? | «So has my aunt, for what she is.» | «Then you are not at all like my aunt.» | «You are the fattest toad I ever saw.» | «Elegant figures don't matter in prison.»
exercise: Why is Toad offended by the plan? | Mr. Toad of Toad Hall disguised as a washerwoman hurts his pride | He thinks the dress will be far too tight and pinch him under the arms | He hates the colour of the rusty black bonnet | He is afraid the aunt will steal his money
exercise: What does the girl say when Toad turns up his nose? | He can stay in prison as a Toad | She will tell her father everything | She will never bring him toast again | She will ask Badger to come and get him
exercise: What is a «coach-and-four»? | A grand carriage pulled by four horses | A coach with four wheels and an engine but no horse at all | A train with four carriages | A team of four teachers
exercise: How does Toad make it up with the girl? | He admits he is a proud and stupid toad | He gives her a gold watch | He sings her a song from his dinner-parties | He promises to marry her aunt
exercise: What settles the bargain with the aunt? | Gold sovereigns, placed where she can see them | A long speech from Toad about Toad Hall | A letter of thanks from the gaoler himself | A promise from Toad to wash her clothes for a year
exercise: What does Toad get for his money? | A cotton print gown, an apron, a shawl and a black bonnet | A policeman's helmet, a cape and a big truncheon | A gaoler's coat, a bunch of keys and a lantern | A fine silk dress, a feathered hat and a fan
exercise: What does the aunt ask to have done to her? | To be gagged and tied up and left in a corner | To be given Toad's best motor-car | To be taken to live at Toad Hall as his housekeeper for ever | To be locked in Toad's cell instead
exercise: Why does she want to be tied up? | So she can say she was attacked and keep her job | Because she enjoys games of make-believe | So that the girl can practise tying all her sailor's knots | Because she is afraid of Toad
exercise: What does «artifice» mean? | A trick | A painting | A fire | A gate
exercise: Why does Toad like the idea of the tied-up aunt? | He keeps his name as a «desperate and dangerous fellow» | It makes him look kind and gentle to everyone | It means he doesn't have to pay her anything at all | It gives him time to eat one more plate of hot buttered toast before he goes
exercise: What does the girl say about how Toad looks in the dress? | He's the very image of her, only more respectable than ever before | He looks nothing like her and will be caught at once | He looks exactly like the Queen of England in her robes | He looks so frightening that the guards will all run away screaming at the sight of him
exercise: What is the biggest danger for Toad on his way out? | The guards' teasing, which he must answer like a washerwoman | The castle's mastiffs, which can smell a toad at once | The gaoler, who is waiting for him at the last gate | The heavy rain, which makes the cotton dress drip and shrink till it tears at the seams
exercise: What is a «wicket-gate»? | A small door set in a big gate | A gate made of cricket bats | A gate for wicked prisoners | A gate that only opens on Friday
exercise: Looking back: how did Toad escape from his own bedroom at Toad Hall in chapter VI? | He knotted his bedsheets into a rope and slid down from the window | He disguised himself as the doctor and walked out of the front door | He hid in the boat-house until Badger had gone to sleep | He climbed up the chimney and ran across the roof
exercise: Looking back: Badger warned in chapter VI that Toad is «at his artfullest» when he seems quiet. Whom did Toad trick then? | The Rat, by pretending to be very ill | The Mole, by hiding in the coal-cellar | Badger, by sending him to the village | The doctor, by paying him to stay away
exercise: Looking back: where did Toad get money for his first escape in chapter VI? | From a small drawer in his dressing-table | From the Rat's coat in the hall | From the chauffeur of the red car | From the inn's till
exercise: ★★★ The aunt brings the washing in on Friday evening. The girl speaks on Thursday, and the aunt comes «next evening». Why is that clever? | Friday evening is when the aunt comes anyway, so nobody wonders | Friday is the gaoler's day off, so he will be out | On Fridays the castle gates are all left wide open | On Fridays the guards are far too busy with their own tea to look at anyone at all
exercise: ★★★ «So has my aunt, for what she is.» What does the girl do with Toad's words? | Turns them back on him, so his boast fits her aunt too | Corrects his grammar and spelling | Tells him he is right and her aunt is ugly | Changes the subject quickly to her canary and her piebald mice
test: Kai from London: «Toad was bare happy to dress as a washerwoman from the start.» (bare = very) Put it right. | At first he was offended; Mr. Toad of Toad Hall in a dress! | Yes, he'd always wanted to wear a gown and bonnet | He was happy, but only if he could also wear a golden crown and a long red cloak | He never wore the dress; the aunt wore his clothes
test: «The aunt was wicked angry and fought Toad for her dress.» (wicked = really) Put it right. | She agreed for gold, and asked to be tied up as a pretend | She called the guards and Toad was caught at once | She ran off with Toad's money and kept the dress | She fought hard, but Toad tied her up with his bedsheets and took the dress anyway
test: Which is TRUE about the plan? | It was the girl's idea, and her aunt agreed for money | It was Badger's idea, sent in a secret letter baked inside a loaf | It was the gaoler's idea, to get rid of Toad | It was Toad's idea from the very first day
test: What does Toad's rude joke about aunts show about him? | He looks down on working people, an old snobbish idea | He has many aunts who really do the washing for the whole of Toad Hall | He loves washing and wants to learn | He is shy and can't think of anything to say
test: Rhys from Swansea, tamping (furious): «that girl's horrible, she shouts at Toad!» (tamping = furious) What's fairer? | She is kind and clever; she snaps only because Toad is rude and proud | Yes, she hates Toad and wants him to stay in prison | She shouts because she is the real prison governor | She never speaks to Toad at all, not one single word, in all those weeks
test: «Proper job, Toad, you're humble now for ever.» (proper job = well done) Is that likely, from what we know of him? | No: he admits he was wrong, but his pride always comes back | Yes: from now on he is the humblest animal alive | Yes: he never boasts again in the whole book | No: he never admitted anything at all, not even to the girl who helped him
test: What would happen if Toad lost his temper with the joking guards? | They might see he wasn't the washerwoman and catch him | They would open the gates even faster | They would laugh and give him a free ride home in the prison cart | Nothing at all; guards never notice anything
test: «Toad's got rizz, the guards all love him.» (rizz = charm) Why are the guards really so friendly? | They think he is the washerwoman they all know | They know he is Toad and like him | Toad paid each of them a sovereign | The girl told every one of them to be nice to her aunt today
test: Why does the book say Toad felt «a little humbled»? | His welcome was really the washerwoman's, not his own | The guards told him he was the ugliest washerwoman they had ever seen | He fell over the hem of his dress | He was caught and sent back
test: Looking back: in chapter VI, how did Toad get out of his bedroom? | He knotted the sheets and slid down from the Tudor window | He walked out dressed as the village doctor | He hid in a big laundry basket that Badger carried out without knowing | He dug through the floor into the dining-hall
test: Looking back: Rat said in chapter II that Toad «may be both boastful and conceited», but has «great qualities». Which good quality shows here? | Honest Toad is ready to admit himself in the wrong | He drives well | He is very quiet and never talks | He never spends any money
test: Looking back: who said in chapter VI, «It's for your own good, Toady, you know» as Toad was hauled upstairs? | The Rat | Badger | The Mole | The gaoler
test: Looking back: at the start of chapter VIII, Toad called his friends wise and clever. Has he stopped boasting by now? | No: with a little comfort his pride is back, as with the figure joke | Yes: he never says anything about himself now | Yes: he only talks about the girl's canary | No: he boasts all day that he will drive the castle's car out through the front gate
test: ★★★ The washing goes out on Monday morning and comes back on Friday evening. Not counting Monday and Friday, for how many whole days is it away? | Three | Two | Four | Five
test: ★★★ Careful: who is gagged and bound in the cell when Toad leaves? | The washerwoman aunt | The gaoler's daughter | The old gaoler | Nobody at all
exam: Identify the means of escape proposed to the prisoner by the gaoler's daughter. | The assumption of the dress and character of the castle washerwoman | The bribery of the sentries at each of the castle gates in succession | The excavation of a passage beneath the walls of the keep | The descent from a window by means of knotted bedclothes
exam: Ascertain the consideration in return for which the washerwoman surrendered her garments. | A sum in gold sovereigns, placed upon the table in full view | A promise of permanent employment at Toad Hall | The prisoner's watch | Nothing whatsoever, she being his aunt
exam: State the stipulation made by the washerwoman in respect of the arrangement. | That she should be gagged, bound and deposited in a corner | That the prisoner should return her garments within one week | That the gaoler should be informed of the matter in advance | That her niece should accompany the prisoner to the outer gate
exam: Which of the following statements is consistent with the established facts concerning the prisoner's passage through the castle? | He was subjected to chaff by the warders, to which he replied without losing his temper | He was recognised at the first gate and returned forthwith to his cell | He passed through every gate in silence, no single word being addressed to him by any warder | He was escorted to the outer gate by the gaoler in person
